encyclopedia :eshkul do
  name "Eshkul"
  summary "Eshkuls are long-tailed Korvathi canopy browsers that coat detachable back spines with caustic tree resin and scatter it when cornered."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :animal
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :danger, :subject_planetary_life
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :surface] }
  descriptive_identity appearance: "A heavy climbing body with four gripping feet, a bracing tail and pale back spines stained yellow by resin.", behavior: "Scrapes resin with its lower teeth, combs it through its spines and sleeps draped over broad forks above warm ground.", threat: "Whips resin and detachable needles toward a threat; fresh resin burns exposed skin and can ignite beside a hot weapon."
  log "2026-09-20 — Bristling Hydra suggested a visibly replenished defense: https://mtg.wtf/card/klr/156/Bristling-Hydra. Eshkul defenses depend on gathered plant resin and shed spines; the species has no magical ward or energy-counter analogue."

  prose <<~PROSE
    Eshkuls browse fruit and tender bark in Korvath's warm upland forests. An adult can rear to a person's shoulder but usually hangs beneath a branch with its long tail braced above. Four gripping feet let it turn on the trunk while keeping its back toward an approaching animal. Pale spines lie flat along the back until the animal is alarmed.

    The species gathers resin from wounded trees with a grooved lower tooth and combs it through those spines. The coating deters biting insects and makes a bite into the animal painful. A cornered eshkul raises its spines, turns sideways and lashes its back against the nearest branch. Droplets and loose needles scatter beyond the reach of its claws. Eshkuls is the usual plural; keepers call an animal freshly coated when the yellow resin still runs between its spines.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :biology, heading: "A Spent Defense"
    A repeated display uses up the coating and loosens more needles. The animal then tries to climb above the threat or retreat to a resin-bearing trunk. Shed spines grow back slowly. Young animals with sparse coats stay near adults and flee before displaying. A bare-backed adult spends more time feeding under cover and is vulnerable to predators while crossing open ground.

    Fresh resin damages eyes and soft skin. Dry needles remain hazardous to handle. Oil loosens the resin before washing; scrubbing with water alone spreads it over a wider patch of skin. Fire is dangerous to the animal as well as its pursuers. A hot muzzle or burning branch can set its coat alight, and the frightened animal carries flame into adjoining foliage.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :people, heading: "Animals That Return"
    Hand-raised eshkuls recognize feeders, follow familiar tapping sounds and tolerate a keeper beneath their branch. Some return to sleeping forks beside a house after feeding in the wild. They can still spray someone they recognize when restrained or startled. Keepers approach with cloth over their eyes and let an animal choose whether to descend.

    Artists collect fallen needles to scrape dried resin for varnish. Hunters sometimes provoke a display over a sheet to gather fresh coating. The sheet can catch needles while leaving the people holding it within range of the droplets. Feeding an animal outside a house draws wild rivals toward the same tree.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :present_day, heading: "The Iridess Keepers"
    The #{encyclopedia_ref :ulessa} canopy at #{ref :iridess} supplies resin and sleeping boughs. #{ref :reth}, familiar to households around #{ref :naloven}, is one of the animals that returns to people without remaining confined. Its toleration of a known feeder does not extend to every visitor following that feeder up a tree.

    The #{ref :ilvaren, "Ilvaren"} teach rescuers to clear a retreat before approaching an exhausted eshkul. Their clean #{encyclopedia_ref :teshil} loosens adhering resin after a display, while collectors carry shed spines away for #{encyclopedia_ref :ulessa_amber}. Keeping the resin harvest separate from live handling avoids provoking another display beside an already injured animal.
  PROSE
  cue "A long tail tightens around the branch while yellow-stained back spines lift toward the approaching footsteps."
  cue "A bare-backed adult scrapes a resin wound with its lower teeth and combs the wet coating along its remaining needles."
  affordance "A climbing eshkul exposes the trunk beyond its defended back; a quiet party can retreat around the far side while it watches their abandoned position."
  pressure "A fresh resin display beside a hot weapon can ignite the animal's coat and carry fire into the canopy."
  variation "Animals around hot springs remain high above gas hollows; orchard visitors sleep lower and become familiar with household noises."
  variation "A heavily coated adult stands its ground; one that has spent its needles retreats and seeks bark resin before returning."
end
